    "rationale": "Selection rationale: We use coco2017_det_train as a single coherent backbone dataset to cover all three target classes (person, dog, cat) in indoor/outdoor photography scenarios. Coco 2017 provides robust, large-scale, consistently annotated detections across these classes, enabling cross-class backbone sharing and stable training on a Mac with Metal acceleration. We target 2,000 images per class (total ~6,000 images across all classes; overlap possible since images may contain multiple classes). Available per-class counts in coco2017_det_train are: person ~64,115; dog ~4,385; cat ~4,114, so 2,000 per class fits comfortably within the respective pools. By keeping to a single backbone family, we maximize domain alignment and annotation consistency, reduce cross-dataset mismatches, and meet the performance/latency constraints (medium latency on Apple Silicon with 16 GB RAM). No other dataset families are mixed to avoid unnecessary complexity and holdout leakage. The final per-class split (training-only pools) will be deterministically split by the trainer, with validation/testing held out from official splits as external benchmarks. The approach satisfies the target ~2,000 positive images per class, maintains a balanced class pool, and stays well within the dataset size constraints (per-class availability) and overall budget.",
